3. Layer 1 — Animal Identification

The first requirement is to ensure that every observation belongs to the correct animal.

RFID-Based Identification

Each cattle is assigned a unique RFID identifier.

When the animal enters a monitored area, the RFID reader identifies it and associates the incoming observations with its digital health profile.

Cow enters monitoring zone
          ↓
RFID detected
          ↓
Animal ID retrieved
          ↓
Sensor + video data linked to animal
          ↓
Animal-specific health record updated
Why this is important

Without reliable animal identification, data from different cows could be mixed, resulting in incorrect risk predictions.

Each animal therefore maintains an individual longitudinal profile containing:

Animal ID
Milk parameters
Milk yield
Behaviour
Rumination/activity
Visual observations
Thermal observations
Historical risk scores
Previous alerts
Veterinary outcomes
4. Layer 2 — Multimodal Data Acquisition

MastiFore collects multiple complementary signals.

A. Milk Parameters

The system can monitor:

Milk temperature
Milk pH
Milk conductivity
Milk yield

These parameters provide information about changes in milk characteristics and production behaviour.

Instead of interpreting one measurement independently, MastiFore observes changes relative to the animal's normal pattern.

B. Behavioural Parameters

Behavioural changes can provide early indications of health abnormalities.

Possible parameters include:

Rumination duration
Activity level
Feeding behaviour
Resting behaviour
Movement patterns
Gait/posture abnormalities

For example:

Normal baseline
     ↓
Gradual reduction in rumination
     +
Reduced activity
     +
Milk yield deviation
     ↓
Increased health-risk evidence
C. CCTV / Computer Vision

Existing farm CCTV cameras can be used wherever camera placement and image quality permit.

The vision pipeline can analyse:

Cow detection
Cow tracking
Body movement
Activity
Gait/posture
Udder-region appearance
Behavioural deviations

The objective is to reuse existing infrastructure rather than requiring specialized smart cameras for every monitoring function.

D. Thermal Imaging

A thermal camera can provide temperature-distribution information, particularly around the udder region.

The system can analyse:

Temperature distribution
Left-right asymmetry
Localized thermal abnormalities
Temporal temperature changes

Thermal information acts as an additional evidence modality, not as a standalone diagnosis.

5. Layer 3 — Data Preprocessing

Raw sensor and video data cannot directly be provided to the AI models.

The preprocessing layer converts raw streams into reliable, synchronized inputs.

Sensor Processing
Raw Sensor Data
      ↓
Noise Removal
      ↓
Outlier Detection
      ↓
Missing Data Handling
      ↓
Calibration / Normalization
      ↓
Temporal Alignment
      ↓
Feature Extraction

Possible features include:

Current value
Moving average
Rate of change
Deviation from baseline
Short-term trend
Long-term trend
Video Processing
Video Stream
     ↓
Frame Extraction
     ↓
Cow Detection
     ↓
Cow Tracking
     ↓
Animal-ID Association
     ↓
Region of Interest Extraction
     ↓
Feature Extraction

This prevents the AI model from processing the entire farm video unnecessarily.

6. Layer 4 — Animal-Specific Baseline

One of the important architectural improvements is to avoid treating every cow as identical.

Different cattle can naturally have different:

Milk yields
Conductivity values
Activity levels
Rumination patterns
Body temperatures

Therefore, MastiFore can maintain an individual baseline for each animal.

Example

Instead of:

Conductivity > X = abnormal

the system can consider:

Conductivity is significantly higher than this cow's established normal range.

Similarly:

Current rumination is substantially below the animal's recent baseline.

This enables personalized anomaly detection.

7. Layer 5 — Modality-Specific AI

Each data type is first processed by a model appropriate to that modality.

Sensor / Time-Series Model

Inputs:

pH
Conductivity
Temperature
Milk yield

The model learns temporal relationships and identifies deviations from normal patterns.

Output:

Sensor-based risk score

Behaviour Model

Inputs:

Activity
Rumination
Feeding
Movement

The model identifies behavioural deviations.

Output:

Behavioural risk score

Computer Vision Model

Inputs:

CCTV frames
Udder-region images
Movement sequences

The model extracts visual abnormalities and behavioural cues.

Output:

Vision-based risk score

Thermal Model

Inputs:

Thermal frames
Udder temperature distribution

The model identifies thermal anomalies and asymmetry.

Output:

Thermal risk score

8. Layer 6 — Multimodal Fusion Engine

This is the central intelligence layer of MastiFore.

A single parameter can produce false alarms.

For example:

High conductivity
       ↓
Could indicate abnormality
       OR
Could be measurement/environmental variation

Instead, MastiFore combines independent evidence.

Sensor AI ─────────┐
                   │
Behaviour AI ──────┤
                   │
Vision AI ─────────┤──→ MULTIMODAL FUSION
                   │
Thermal AI ────────┤
                   │
Temporal Analysis ─┘
                         ↓
                  Unified Risk Score

The fusion engine considers:

Individual modality confidence
Agreement between modalities
Temporal consistency
Animal-specific baseline
Missing modalities
Model uncertainty
Important principle

One abnormal signal should not automatically mean mastitis.

Confidence should increase when multiple independent modalities show consistent evidence.

9. Layer 7 — Risk Assessment

The fusion engine produces a continuous risk estimate.

For example:

                RISK SCORE
                   │
      0 ──────────┼────────── 100
                   │
       LOW         │   HIGH
        │          │      │
      0–30       31–60   61–100

These ranges are illustrative. Final thresholds should be determined using validation data and operating requirements.

The system should provide both:

Risk

How strongly the available evidence indicates an abnormal condition.

Confidence

How reliable the prediction is based on:

Data quality
Model confidence
Number of available modalities
Cross-modal agreement

This distinction is important.

A system may have:

High risk + low confidence

if only one noisy modality is available.

10. Layer 8 — Three-Tier Decision Engine

The three-tier architecture is used to balance early detection and false-alarm control.

TIER 1 — EARLY SCREENING
Objective

Identify cattle that show early deviations from their normal state.

Inputs

Primarily low-cost/continuously available signals:

Milk parameters
Milk yield
Activity
Rumination
Behaviour
Output
Normal
   ↓
Continue monitoring

Suspicious
   ↓
Escalate to Tier 2

Tier 1 is intentionally designed as a screening stage, not a diagnosis.

TIER 2 — MULTIMODAL VALIDATION

When Tier 1 detects suspicious behaviour, additional evidence is analysed.

Possible inputs:

CCTV
Udder-region vision
Thermal imaging
Behavioural trends
Sensor history

The system checks whether the signals are consistent.

Example
Conductivity ↑
Milk yield ↓
Rumination ↓
       +
Thermal asymmetry ↑
       ↓
Cross-modal agreement
       ↓
Higher confidence

But:

Conductivity ↑
       +
Normal behaviour
       +
Normal thermal pattern
       ↓
Low cross-modal agreement
       ↓
Continue monitoring / lower confidence

This stage helps reduce unnecessary alerts.

TIER 3 — VETERINARY VALIDATION

Tier 3 is the final escalation stage.

The system prepares an evidence summary for the veterinarian.

Veterinary evidence report
Animal ID: C-024

Risk: HIGH

Observed deviations:
• Milk conductivity ↑
• Milk yield ↓
• Rumination ↓
• Thermal asymmetry detected

Trend:
Abnormality increasing over previous observations

AI recommendation:
Veterinary examination recommended

The veterinarian then performs appropriate clinical examination and/or confirmatory testing.

The AI system does not replace veterinary diagnosis.

11. Layer 9 — Alert & Communication System

The output should be converted into an actionable alert.

Farmer Dashboard

The dashboard provides:

Overall herd health
Individual animal status
Risk level
Parameter trends
Historical alerts
Animal health timeline
Veterinary recommendations/status
Local-Language SMS

Small-scale farmers may not continuously monitor a dashboard.

Therefore, high-priority alerts can also be delivered through SMS in the farmer's preferred language.

Example:

Cow C-024 shows a high risk of mastitis. Veterinary examination is recommended.

This makes the system accessible beyond users who actively use a web dashboard.

12. Layer 10 — Veterinary Feedback Loop

The architecture should not stop after generating an alert.

The veterinary outcome becomes valuable labelled information.

AI Risk Prediction
        ↓
Veterinary Examination
        ↓
┌───────────────┐
│   Outcome     │
├───────────────┤
│ Confirmed     │
│ Not confirmed │
│ Follow-up     │
└───────┬───────┘
        ↓
Store outcome
        ↓
Build labelled dataset
        ↓
Model evaluation / retraining
        ↓
Improved future predictions

This creates a closed-loop learning architecture.

13. Central Data Layer

All observations are stored against the animal's unique ID.

Suggested data structure
Animal
 ├── Identification
 ├── Sensor Records
 ├── Milk Records
 ├── Behaviour Records
 ├── Video Features
 ├── Thermal Features
 ├── Risk Scores
 ├── Alerts
 └── Veterinary Outcomes

This allows longitudinal analysis rather than isolated prediction.

14. End-to-End Workflow
Step 1 — Identify the Animal

Cow enters the monitored zone.

↓

RFID reader identifies the animal.

↓

System loads the animal's historical profile.

Step 2 — Collect Data

The system collects available:

Milk parameters
Milk yield
Behaviour
Rumination/activity
CCTV observations
Thermal observations
Step 3 — Preprocess

Raw data is:

Cleaned
Filtered
Normalized
Time-aligned
Associated with the correct animal
Step 4 — Compare Against Baseline

Current observations are compared with:

Animal's historical baseline
Recent trends
Relevant population-level patterns

↓

Abnormal deviations are identified.

Step 5 — Run AI Models

Different AI models analyse different modalities.

Milk/Sensor ──→ Sensor Model ──→ Risk
Behaviour ────→ Behaviour Model → Risk
CCTV ─────────→ Vision Model ───→ Risk
Thermal ──────→ Thermal Model ──→ Risk
Step 6 — Fuse Evidence

The fusion engine combines model outputs.

↓

Produces:

Overall risk + confidence + supporting evidence

Step 7 — Tier 1 Screening

If the risk is low:

Continue routine monitoring.

If suspicious:

Escalate to Tier 2.

Step 8 — Tier 2 Validation

Additional modalities are analysed.

↓

If evidence is inconsistent:

Continue monitoring / lower-confidence alert.

If evidence is consistent:

Escalate to Tier 3.

Step 9 — Veterinary Escalation

The system generates an evidence-based veterinary alert.

↓

Veterinarian examines the animal.

↓

Clinical/confirmatory result is recorded.

Step 10 — Feedback

The confirmed outcome is stored.

↓

The outcome becomes labelled information for:

Performance evaluation
Threshold optimization
Dataset expansion
Future model improvement
